<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The curve <m>y = {-6 x^2+36 x-30}</m> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at two points.</p><p>(a) Find the smaller <m>x</m>-coordinate.</p><div><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the larger <m>x</m>-coordinate.</p><div><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the exact area enclosed by the curve and the <m>x</m>-axis.</p><div>Area <m>=</m> <fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/> square units  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Sketch first. The curve is an upside-down parabola, above the <m>x</m>-axis between its two intercepts, so the enclosed area is the integral between them.</p><p>(a), (b) <m>{-6 x^2+36 x-30} = {-6 \left(x-5\right) \left(x-1\right)}</m>, so <m>y = 0</m> when <m>x = {1}</m> or <m>x = {5}</m>.</p><p>(c) <me>\int_{ {1} }^{ {5} } \left({-6 x^2+36 x-30}\right)dx = \left[{-2 x^3+18 x^2-30 x}\right]_{ {1} }^{ {5} } = {50} - \left({-14}\right) = {64}</me></p><p>The area is <m>{64}</m> square units.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>64</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>